      <div class="osa-answer-grid">
        <div class="osa-answer-block"><h3>Osaki vs. Kyota</h3><p>Kyota offers a focused lineup with their distinctive Syner-D dual-roller technology. Kyota tends to feel more refined and slightly more moderate in intensity. Choose Osaki for feature breadth and stronger intensity; consider Kyota if you prefer a more polished, less aggressive feel with innovative roller design.</p></div>
        <div class="osa-answer-block"><h3>Osaki vs. Infinity</h3><p>Infinity offers competitive pricing and solid 4D technology. The feel tends toward moderate — comfortable rather than deeply assertive. Osaki generally edges Infinity on feature density at comparable prices in the mid-to-upper range, particularly in the Duo/dual-mechanism category.</p></div>
        <div class="osa-answer-block"><h3>Osaki vs. Ogawa</h3><p>Ogawa differentiates more strongly on design and the overall luxury experience. The Ogawa Master Drive Duo is a direct competitor to the Osaki DuoMax tier. If design integration matters as much as performance, Ogawa deserves a side-by-side comparison.</p></div>
        <div class="osa-answer-block"><h3>Osaki vs. Bodyfriend</h3><p>Where Osaki delivers feature density and intensity, Bodyfriend delivers a more refined, precision-engineering experience through its proprietary XD Pro roller module. Bodyfriend starts at a higher price floor (~$9K). Osaki for assertive performance and wide choice; Bodyfriend for crafted, design-led luxury feel.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

